WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.666
This paper, from KU Leuven and presented at ICML 2023,

00:00:04.746 --> 00:00:08.912
revisits Kernel Principal Component Analysis
through the lens of convex duality.

00:00:08.992 --> 00:00:13.158
The authors reformulate Kernel PCA as a difference
of convex functions problem,

00:00:13.238 --> 00:00:17.404
then dualize it.

00:00:17.484 --> 00:00:21.650
This single move unlocks three things at once:
gradient-based solvers that avoid the expensive

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:25.896
cubic singular value decomposition of the Gram matrix,

00:00:25.976 --> 00:00:30.142
a flexible framework that swaps in robust or sparse
objectives through Moreau envelopes,

00:00:30.222 --> 00:00:34.388
and significant speedups on real benchmarks.

00:00:34.468 --> 00:00:38.634
It is a clean example of how the right optimization
viewpoint can extend a classical method

00:00:38.714 --> 00:00:42.880
in several directions simultaneously.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:47.622
Kernel PCA is one of the most widely used tools in
unsupervised learning,

00:00:47.703 --> 00:00:52.065
but it has a stubborn scaling problem.

00:00:52.145 --> 00:00:56.508
The standard recipe computes the singular value
decomposition of the n-by-n Gram matrix,

00:00:56.588 --> 00:01:00.950
which costs order n-cubed and becomes painfully
slow even for moderately sized datasets.

00:01:01.030 --> 00:01:05.393
On top of that,

00:01:05.473 --> 00:01:09.835
every time researchers wanted a robust or a sparse
variant of Kernel PCA,

00:01:09.915 --> 00:01:14.278
they reached for a different ad-hoc formulation or
weighting heuristic,

00:01:14.358 --> 00:01:18.720
producing a scattered collection of unrelated
optimization problems rather than one coherent method.

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:23.671
The authors observe that Kernel PCA can be written
as variance maximization under orthonormality constraints,

00:01:23.751 --> 00:01:28.322
which puts it squarely in the family of
difference-of-convex problems that optimization researchers understand well.

00:01:28.402 --> 00:01:32.973
Earlier work had studied PCA as a
difference-of-convex program,

00:01:33.053 --> 00:01:37.624
but only for linear PCA,

00:01:37.704 --> 00:01:42.276
and often only for the first component where the
orthogonality constraints vanish.

00:01:42.356 --> 00:01:46.927
Meanwhile,

00:01:47.007 --> 00:01:51.578
a separate line of research had shown that infimal
convolution is an elegant way to build robust or sparse losses,

00:01:51.658 --> 00:01:56.229
and that these constructions behave especially
nicely in dual formulations.

00:01:56.309 --> 00:02:00.880
Bringing these two observations together is the
opening the paper exploits.

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:05.202
The core contribution is a general dual-based
formulation of Kernel PCA built on the dualization

00:02:05.282 --> 00:02:09.223
of a difference of convex functions.

00:02:09.303 --> 00:02:13.245
This formulation does two things at once.

00:02:13.325 --> 00:02:17.267
First, it turns the problem into one over a
finite-dimensional dual matrix,

00:02:17.347 --> 00:02:21.288
which can be solved with efficient gradient-based
methods and avoids the expensive singular value decomposition,

00:02:21.368 --> 00:02:25.310
even when the feature space is infinite-dimensional.

00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:29.332
Second,

00:02:29.412 --> 00:02:33.353
it makes the objective modular: by choosing
objectives expressible as Moreau envelopes,

00:02:33.433 --> 00:02:37.375
the same framework promotes robustness or sparsity
without leaving the dual picture.

00:02:37.455 --> 00:02:41.397
The authors supply the supporting theory,

00:02:41.477 --> 00:02:45.418
concrete L-BFGS and difference-of-convex algorithms,

00:02:45.498 --> 00:02:49.440
and the proximal operators needed to make the
robust and sparse cases practical.

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:55.539
The method starts by writing Kernel PCA as the
minimization of a difference of two convex

00:02:55.619 --> 00:02:59.318
functions over the Stiefel manifold of orthonormal frames.

00:02:59.398 --> 00:03:03.748
A general dualization proposition then converts
this primal problem,

00:03:03.828 --> 00:03:08.309
whose variables live in a possibly
infinite-dimensional feature space,

00:03:08.389 --> 00:03:12.414
into a dual problem whose variable H is a finite
n-by-s matrix,

00:03:12.494 --> 00:03:14.824
perfectly suited to gradient descent.

00:03:14.904 --> 00:03:19.124
The indicator function of the Stiefel manifold
turns, in the dual,

00:03:19.204 --> 00:03:22.382
into a nuclear-norm term the authors call pi of H,

00:03:22.462 --> 00:03:26.096
equal to the trace of the square root of H-transpose G H.

00:03:26.176 --> 00:03:31.634
Its gradient only requires the singular value
decomposition of a small s-by-s matrix,

00:03:31.714 --> 00:03:36.064
not the full n-by-n Gram matrix, which is the
source of the speedup.

00:03:36.144 --> 00:03:38.736
For the standard problem they run L-BFGS.

00:03:38.816 --> 00:03:40.951
To promote robustness or sparsity,

00:03:41.031 --> 00:03:47.466
they add a Moreau-envelope term to the objective
and optimize with a difference-of-convex algorithm,

00:03:47.546 --> 00:03:53.200
plugging in closed-form proximal operators for the
Huber and epsilon-insensitive losses.

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:57.026
The experiments span synthetic and real-world data.

00:03:57.106 --> 00:04:02.488
For efficiency, the authors time Kernel PCA on a
synthetic 7,000-point problem,

00:04:02.568 --> 00:04:05.600
the Protein dataset with about 15,000 points,

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:11.476
the RCV1 text collection with roughly 20,000
points, and CIFAR-10 with 60,000 images.

00:04:11.556 --> 00:04:18.805
Robustness is tested by artificially contaminating
the Iris dataset with multiplicative Gaussian outliers,

00:04:18.885 --> 00:04:24.820
and sparsity is measured through reconstruction
error under epsilon-insensitive losses.

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:31.043
All solvers are compared fairly using a shared
relative dual-cost residual as the stopping

00:04:31.123 --> 00:04:33.394
criterion at two tolerance levels,

00:04:33.474 --> 00:04:36.920
and the timing results are averaged over five runs.

00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:42.077
On the efficiency benchmarks, the proposed solver
wins across the board.

00:04:42.157 --> 00:04:47.811
At the looser tolerance of ten-to-the-minus-two, it
is faster than full SVD, Lanczos,

00:04:47.891 --> 00:04:50.038
and randomized SVD on every task,

00:04:50.118 --> 00:04:53.950
and it is at least three times faster than randomized SVD,

00:04:54.030 --> 00:05:00.089
peaking at a nine-point-one-seven times speedup on
CIFAR-10 with its sixty thousand images,

00:05:00.169 --> 00:05:04.070
where full SVD could not even finish within thirty
minutes.

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:05.419
Just as importantly,

00:05:05.499 --> 00:05:11.355
the method is robust to a property that hurts
randomized SVD badly: as the eigenspectrum

00:05:11.435 --> 00:05:13.919
of the Gram matrix decays more slowly,

00:05:13.999 --> 00:05:16.820
randomized SVD needs many more oversamples,

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:20.800
while the proposed solver's iteration count barely
changes.

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:24.191
Several controlled studies dissect the behavior.

00:05:24.271 --> 00:05:29.986
Sweeping the Huber loss parameter kappa shows that
both Huber variants consistently reduce

00:05:30.066 --> 00:05:35.589
mean squared error on the contaminated Iris data
compared to the ordinary squared loss,

00:05:35.669 --> 00:05:39.581
confirming the robustness the framework is designed
to induce.

00:05:39.661 --> 00:05:45.183
Varying the number of components shows that the
L-BFGS solver keeps its speed advantage

00:05:45.263 --> 00:05:47.759
over randomized SVD across a wide range,

00:05:47.839 --> 00:05:54.198
and only at very large numbers of components does
the per-iteration small SVD start to slow it down.

00:05:54.278 --> 00:06:00.380
And the eigenspectrum study pinpoints the scaling
advantage: as the spectrum decays more slowly,

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:07.850
randomized SVD's oversample count climbs sharply
while the proposed solver's iteration count stays essentially flat.

00:06:08.230 --> 00:06:10.332
A few numbers capture the impact.

00:06:10.412 --> 00:06:16.218
On CIFAR-10 the method delivers a
nine-point-one-seven times speedup over randomized SVD,

00:06:16.298 --> 00:06:21.707
and it is at least three times faster than
randomized SVD on every efficiency task.

00:06:21.787 --> 00:06:29.444
The key complexity change is that each iteration
costs order s-cubed for a small s-by-s singular value decomposition,

00:06:29.524 --> 00:06:34.470
replacing the classical order n-cubed decomposition
of the full Gram matrix.

00:06:34.550 --> 00:06:35.991
On the robustness side,

00:06:36.071 --> 00:06:42.339
the Huber loss cuts the mean squared error on
contaminated Iris from about seven-point-five-nine

00:06:42.419 --> 00:06:46.373
down to six-point-eight-three at the tested
corruption level.

00:06:46.453 --> 00:06:50.539
And these results scale to datasets with sixty
thousand points,

00:06:50.619 --> 00:06:54.110
where full SVD could not finish within thirty minutes.

00:06:54.490 --> 00:07:00.108
The lasting message is that a single change of
viewpoint pays off in three directions at once.

00:07:00.188 --> 00:07:05.018
By writing Kernel PCA as a difference of convex
functions and moving to the dual,

00:07:05.098 --> 00:07:10.352
the authors get a solver that avoids the expensive
singular value decomposition and runs

00:07:10.432 --> 00:07:13.019
up to nine times faster than randomized SVD,

00:07:13.099 --> 00:07:18.293
and in the same breath they gain a modular way to
demand robustness or sparsity just by

00:07:18.373 --> 00:07:20.960
picking the right Moreau-envelope objective.

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:24.294
Because the dual variable is always finite-dimensional,

00:07:24.374 --> 00:07:30.840
the framework even reaches infinite-dimensional
feature maps that classical Kernel PCA simply cannot handle.
